Yet Another Mix Net - A Mixmaster derivative

YAMN tries to look like Mixmaster in order to maintain compatibility with the
various GUI's and peripheral applications that serve to make it a complete
solution.  Under the covers there are some significant differences:-

* Revised crypto functions.  Instead of Mixmaster's Triple-DES, RSAES-PKCS1-v1_5
and MD5, YAMN uses AES, NaCl Box and Blake2.

* Deterministic header padding to protect against tagging attacks

Building from source (needs Go, see go.mod for the version):-
git clone https://github.com/go-while/yamn
cd yamn
go build

Configuration:-
Create a dir for yamn.  (mkdir yamn)
Copy the yamn binary (yamn.exe on Windows) to the above dir
Create yamn.yml in that dir with the options you want to change, starting
from examples/yamn.yml.client or examples/yamn.yml.remailer.
examples/yamn.yml.defaults lists every option with its default.
Run yamn with --dir pointing at that dir, or with --config <dir>/yamn.yml.
Relative paths (the default file names included) are relative to --dir, or
without it to the directory of the --config or $YAMNCFG file.  Otherwise
(~/yamn.yml, /etc/yamn.yml or no config file) they are relative to the
current directory, and yamn warns about it.
yamn --debug prints the configuration in effect.

Examples:-

Send an anonymous email
yamn --mail msg.txt

Use a user-defined chain
yamn --mail --chain="*,*,yamn" msg.txt

Send a single copy (the default is 2)
yamn --mail --copies=1 msg.txt

Keep all headers of the message (by default identifying headers such as
User-Agent, Date and Message-ID are removed, and reported)
yamn --mail --keep-headers msg.txt

Encrypt on one machine, send from another
yamn --mail --stdout msg.txt > out.mbox      (first machine)
yamn --import --send < out.mbox              (second machine)

Perform remailer functions
yamn --remailer

Start a remailer daemon
yamn --remailer --daemon

Hand mail to a remailer from an MTA, or an mbox written by --stdout
yamn --read-mail < message

Keyring updates:-
By default (urls.fetch: true) yamn --mail downloads the keyring and stats
right before sending when they are more than an hour old.  The pingers then
see your IP address and when you send, and a pinger that is down delays the
message by up to 30 s per file.  To keep the downloads apart from sending,
set "urls: fetch: false" in yamn.yml and run yamn --refresh on a schedule,
at a random time, e.g. this crontab line (every 6 hours, up to an hour late):
0 */6 * * * sleep $(shuf -i 0-3600 -n 1); yamn --dir $HOME/yamn --refresh
or a systemd timer with RandomizedDelaySec=1h.  --refresh downloads even
with fetch: false.  A remailer with fetch: false needs such a schedule too,
as it then no longer downloads them every hour.

See doc/yamn.1 for all options, and ISSUES.md for known issues.

Versions:-
This fork (github.com/go-while/yamn) is based on crooks/yamn.  Its
versions are the upstream release plus +gwN, the fork's N-th release on
top of it: 0.2.7+gw1 is the first one based on 0.2.7.  yamn --version, a
remailer's published key and its remailer-conf reply show the full
version.  Encrypted mails only carry the upstream version (yamn-0.2.7),
so mails sent with this fork look the same as mails sent with upstream.
